WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:05.299
For movie lovers, there are a few cinematic
quotes that are always handy for breaking

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:08.320
the ice or amping up the drama of a casual
chat.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:13.880
But many of the most commonly used movie phrases
are almost always misrepresented in conversation.

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:18.360
If you're one of those people who loves to
whip out a famous movie line at an opportune

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:21.720
time, you might want to make sure you're actually
quoting the films correctly.

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:25.640
Here are some of the widely mistaken movie
lines in Hollywood history.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:27.780
"Mirror, mirror"

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:32.140
The world of animation changed the day Walt
Disney and his studio released Snow

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:33.960
White and the Seven Dwarfs.

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:39.100
Certain aspects of this classic resonate with
audiences to this day, such as the poisoned

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:43.899
apple, true love's kiss—and the eternal
question "Mirror, mirror on the wall, who

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:45.340
is the fairest of them all?"

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:48.820
The problem is, no one actually says that
in the film.

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:51.220
What the Evil Queen actually asks is:

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:57.060
"Magic mirror on the wall, who is the fairest
one of all?"

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:02.280
Interestingly enough, in the Grimm brothers'
original story, the Queen says, "Mirror, mirror,

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:05.660
on the wall, who in this land is fairest of
all?"

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:09.520
So, hi-ho, hi-ho off to correct quoting you
go.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:13.880
"Ohhh, you must be Grumpy."

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:16.040
"A bigger boat"

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:22.659
Steven Spielberg pioneered the summer blockbuster
in 1975 with Jaws, his terrifying tale of

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:24.240
a great white serial killer.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:30.540
The eerie cello music, the poster, the characters,
and the quotes—they all made Jaws the classic

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:31.760
movie it is today.

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:36.220
The line that's most often cited from the
movie comes from Chief Brody, who discovers

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:39.160
the deadly creature is much bigger than they're
prepared for.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:43.280
Most people quote his famous line from the
scene to say, "We're gonna need a bigger boat."

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:45.220
But what Brody really says there is:

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:47.760
"You're gonna need a bigger boat."

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:52.120
It's just a one-word difference, but it's
a toothy one even so.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:54.280
"If you build it"

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:59.800
Field of Dreams was both a fantasy film and
a sports drama and is often given tribute

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:01.920
with the line "If you build it, they will
come."

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:07.120
But the truth of the matter is that the whispered
voice over never actually utters that phrase,

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:09.500
and what's actually said instead is:

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:12.980
"If you build it, he will come."

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:20.180
It's not quite as compelling in singular format,
so it's easy to see why people like to use

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:24.850
the "they" instead of "he," but if you're
looking for something more inclusive to spout

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:27.200
out, you can always use this line instead:

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:29.300
"People will come."

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:31.500
"Run, you fools"

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:36.520
Peter Jackson's Lord of the Rings trilogy
was a ground-breaking adaptation of J.R.R.

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:40.920
Tolkien's beloved novels and boasts an abundance
of quotable moments in the movies.

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:42.200
"PO-TAY-TOES"

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:46.540
The one that seems to be most-often mistaken
is from The Fellowship of the Rings.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:51.500
Gandalf lovers often like to say "Run, you
fools!" but in reality, what our old wizard

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:53.560
pal actually says in the movie is:

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:55.420
"Fly, you fools!"

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:58.960
Why would he say that when none of his traveling
companions had wings?

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:01.740
Well, "fly" in old English means to flee.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:06.140
Orrrrr maybe he was just foreshadowing the
arrival of the Eagles in movie three.

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:08.340
"Feel lucky, punk?"

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:13.120
No matter how many movies Clint Eastwood directs
or stars in, his fans will always remember

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:18.231
him as the Man with No Name from the spaghetti
Western scene and Harry Callahan from the

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:19.480
Dirty Harry series.

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:24.180
But as quotable as the latter character is,
fans often butcher his biggest line.

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:28.480
It's much easier to shorten the scene to state,
simply, "Do you feel lucky, punk?" when the

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:31.110
time is right, but what he actually says is:

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:34.500
"You've got to ask yourself one question:
'Do I feel lucky?'

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:37.080
Well, do ya, punk?"

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:41.280
Now that you know the proper dialogue, you
should feel lucky.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:43.240
"Play it again"

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:48.060
Casablanca is one of the most cherished films
in American history and is known for being

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:49.340
exceptionally quotable.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:51.800
You can thank the movie for this epic goodbye
line:

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:53.920
"Here's looking at you, kid."

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:55.700
And this go-to party toast:

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:58.960
"I think this is the beginning of a beautiful
friendship."

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:01.040
And let's not forget this lover's lament:

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:07.000
"Of all the gin joints in all the towns in
the world, she walks into mine."

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:11.580
But people tend to get it wrong when they
quote the film to say, "Play it again, Sam."

00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:15.760
The misquote even became the title of Woody
Allen's 1972 comedy film.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:19.520
But what Ilsa actually says upon walking into
the cafe is

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:22.940
"Play it once, Sam, for old time's sake."

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:24.180
"Play it, Sam.

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:27.460
Play 'As Time Goes By.'"

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:29.240
And when Rick wants to hear it again?

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:30.940
Well, this is what he really says.

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:33.060
"You played it for her you can play it for
me."

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:34.580
"Well I don't think I can…"

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:36.240
"If she can stand it I can stand it.

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:37.580
. PLAY IT!"

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:40.260
Hey, everybody makes mistakes!

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:42.480
"I am your father"

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:47.900
Star Wars is perhaps the biggest entertainment
franchise in the world—and it doesn't seem

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:49.600
to be stopping any time soon.

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:55.139
Aside from all the lightsabers and galaxy-saving,
the series contains a plethora of famous lines,

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:58.760
from "may the Force be with you" to "I have
a bad feeling about this."

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:03.140
The line that seems to get mixed up most often
is when people quote Darth Vader to tell Luke

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:05.600
Skywalker, "Luke, I am your father."

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:09.410
But that's a line straight out of the dark
side of audience's memories because what Vader

00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:10.880
really says is:

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:11.620
"No.

00:05:13.220 --> 00:05:14.580
I am your father!"

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:18.540
Now that you know the truth, the Force can
really be with you.

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:20.840
"Not in Kansas"

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:24.740
It only makes sense that one of the most famous
quotes in history comes from one of the most

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:27.440
iconic films in history: The Wizard of Oz.

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:32.060
When Dorothy arrives in Oz thanks to a tornado,
most people quote her to tell her dog Toto,

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:34.180
"I don't think we're in Kansas anymore."

00:05:34.409 --> 00:05:38.580
But that must be some Wicked Witch wizardry
at play because what our ruby shoe-sporting

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:40.160
pal actually says is:

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:45.180
"Toto, I've a feeling we're not in Kansas
anymore."

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:48.250
If you've ever misread that line aloud, don't
worry.

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:49.960
At least you're not the scarecrow.

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:51.760
Thanks for watching!

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:54.400
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:56.920
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

